  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nOther than an increased chance of showers and thunderstorms across \nthe Victoria Crossroads and over the Coastal Waters, T.S. Edouard is \nexpected to have little to no impacts on S TX. \n\nA mid level high pressure system centered over the Southeast U.S. \nwill remain quasi-stationary over the next couple of days, with high \npressure briefly building across S TX as the mid level low \nassociated with T.S. Edouard moves northwest away from the region. \nModels prog high pressure to dominate a majority of the Central U.S. \nthrough Labor Day weekend. This will allow mid level troughs of low \npressure to move around the southern periphery of the high pressure \nand across S TX. PWATs are progged to range between 1.8-2.1 inches \nover the weekend, which will be sufficient for isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms to develop along the daily sea\nbreeze. The best chance of precip will be across the Victoria \nCrossroads with up to a 45% chance. There is a low (10-30%) of \nconvection daily along the sea breeze Monday and Tuesday, as well.\n\nDespite an increase in rain chances over the weekend, heat will \ncontinue to be a threat with heat indices of 105-109 area-wide and \nbriefly up to 114 across a few locations at times. There is a major \nto extreme risk of heat related impacts across the inland Coastal \nPlains to the Rio Grande Plains through the period. A moderate risk \nis expected across the Victoria Crossroads.",
